======================================================================================================================
L198 NGC 1052-DF2: the external field effect and the universal retained fraction, against the measured dispersion
======================================================================================================================
    inputs: at 20 Mpc, stellar mass 2.0e8 Msun, R_e = 2.2 kpc, projected separation from NGC 1052 = 80 kpc; all three scale with the assumed distance.
    measured stellar dispersion: 8.5 +/- 2.5 km/s and 10.8 +/- 3.5 km/s
    --- distance 20 Mpc: stellar mass 2.00e+08, R_e = 2.20 kpc, separation 80 kpc
      [canonical] stars only               retained sector 0.00e+00 Msun | g_int/a0 = 0.062, g_ext/a0 = 0.164, boost = 3.00 | sigma =  12.1 km/s with the external field,  14.9 isolated
      [canonical] stars + retained sector  retained sector 1.73e+08 Msun | g_int/a0 = 0.115, g_ext/a0 = 0.164, boost = 3.00 | sigma =  16.5 km/s with the external field,  17.8 isolated
      [alt      ] stars only               retained sector 0.00e+00 Msun | g_int/a0 = 0.051, g_ext/a0 = 0.149, boost = 3.12 | sigma =  12.4 km/s with the external field,  15.5 isolated
      [alt      ] stars + retained sector  retained sector 1.73e+08 Msun | g_int/a0 = 0.095, g_ext/a0 = 0.149, boost = 3.12 | sigma =  16.9 km/s with the external field,  18.5 isolated
    --- distance 13 Mpc: stellar mass 8.45e+07, R_e = 1.43 kpc, separation 52 kpc
      [canonical] stars only               retained sector 0.00e+00 Msun | g_int/a0 = 0.062, g_ext/a0 = 0.263, boost = 2.49 | sigma =   8.9 km/s with the external field,  12.0 isolated
      [canonical] stars + retained sector  retained sector 5.68e+07 Msun | g_int/a0 = 0.103, g_ext/a0 = 0.263, boost = 2.49 | sigma =  11.5 km/s with the external field,  13.9 isolated
      [alt      ] stars only               retained sector 0.00e+00 Msun | g_int/a0 = 0.051, g_ext/a0 = 0.237, boost = 2.59 | sigma =   9.1 km/s with the external field,  12.5 isolated
      [alt      ] stars + retained sector  retained sector 5.68e+07 Msun | g_int/a0 = 0.085, g_ext/a0 = 0.237, boost = 2.59 | sigma =  11.7 km/s with the external field,  14.5 isolated
  [PASS] V1 [the external field effect is operating] at the far distance the neighbour's field exceeds DF2's own internal field on both footings, so the boost is set by NGC 1052 rather than by DF2: this object is in the regime where a kernel and a dark halo make structurally different predictions, which is what makes it a test at all
           (canonical, sector=False: g_ext/a0 = 0.164 vs g_int/a0 = 0.062; canonical, sector=True: g_ext/a0 = 0.164 vs g_int/a0 = 0.115)
  [PASS] V2 [how much the external field actually does -- less than I assumed] it lowers the predicted dispersion by about 20%, not by a factor: both the internal and the external acceleration are well below a0, so the object is deep in the modified regime either way and the neighbour only softens the boost rather than switching it off
           (canonical: isolated 14.9 vs external-field 12.1 km/s; alt: isolated 15.5 vs external-field 12.4 km/s)
    tension with each measurement (positive means the prediction is too hot):
      [canonical] stars only       sigma =  12.1 km/s -> +1.4 sigma vs Danieli, +0.4 sigma vs Emsellem
      [canonical] stars + sector   sigma =  16.5 km/s -> +3.2 sigma vs Danieli, +1.6 sigma vs Emsellem
      [alt      ] stars only       sigma =  12.4 km/s -> +1.5 sigma vs Danieli, +0.4 sigma vs Emsellem
      [alt      ] stars + sector   sigma =  16.9 km/s -> +3.4 sigma vs Danieli, +1.7 sigma vs Emsellem
  [PASS] V3 [stars alone are fine] with the external field and no retained sector the prediction sits within about one and a half standard deviations of both stellar measurements on both footings
           (canonical: 12.1 km/s; alt: 12.4 km/s)
  [PASS] V4 [THE TEST OF THE UNIVERSAL FLOOR] adding the 13.5% retained sector that L191 predicts for EVERY galaxy pushes the prediction above both measurements by more than two standard deviations at the far distance: DF2 is in tension with the universal floor, and that is a falsifiable statement rather than a soft one
           (canonical: 16.5 km/s, +3.2 sigma vs Danieli and +1.6 vs Emsellem; alt: 16.9 km/s, +3.4 sigma vs Danieli and +1.7 vs Emsellem)
  [PASS] V5 [the distance is the escape hatch, quantified] at the nearer disputed distance the same retained sector falls within two standard deviations of the measurements, so the tension is a statement about the far distance and cannot be settled until the distance is
           (canonical at 13 Mpc: 11.5 km/s, +1.2 sigma vs Danieli; alt at 13 Mpc: 11.7 km/s, +1.3 sigma vs Danieli)
  [PASS] V6 [a heavier neighbour RELIEVES the tension -- I had this backwards] the kernel weakens as the acceleration rises, so a more massive NGC 1052 means a larger external field, a SMALLER boost and a lower predicted dispersion: tripling the neighbour's mass drops the retained-sector prediction by about 2 km/s, which is a second escape hatch alongside the distance
           (with a 3e11 neighbour: 14.7 15.0 km/s against 16.5 16.9)
    READING: the external field effect is doing real work here and the kernel needs it. What DF2 puts under strain is not the kernel but the UNIVERSAL RETAINED
    FRACTION that L191 predicts, and only at the far distance. Two escapes remain, both stated rather than assumed: the distance is disputed, and a satellite this
    close to a giant can be tidally stripped below any universal floor.
    LIMITS: half-mass estimator sigma^2 = G M/(8 R_e), isotropic and spherical; the simple external-field prescription, boost = nu(g_ext/a0), with no directional
    factor (the full treatment reduces the boost somewhat, which would lower every number here); the retained sector's profile assumed NFW with c = 10 and its halo
    mass from abundance matching; projected separation used as the true one, so the external field is an upper bound; no tidal stripping modelled.

